Fair point.

18:54.420 --> 18:59.140
How is the universe physically allowed to retain a coherent memory over cosmological timescales?

18:59.740 --> 19:03.400
This is where Flyxion has to deploy some deeply foundational mathematics.

19:04.360 --> 19:06.960
To prove that the memory isn't permanently lost to entropy,

19:07.120 --> 19:11.220
the paper invokes Poincaré's recurrence theorem and Kac's lemma.

19:11.680 --> 19:12.520
Let's unpack those.

19:12.620 --> 19:14.760
What did Henri Poincaré prove?

19:14.760 --> 19:17.740
Poincaré proved a theorem in the late 19th century,

19:18.100 --> 19:23.820
stating that in any bounded physical system with a finite amount of energy and finite entropy,

19:24.480 --> 19:26.680
the system will eventually, inevitably,

19:27.380 --> 19:31.060
return to a state that is arbitrarily close to its initial starting point.

19:31.060 --> 19:33.540
Okay, give us a visual for that.

19:34.180 --> 19:37.560
Think of a perfectly sealed glass box filled with gas molecules.

19:38.040 --> 19:40.360
The molecules bounce around in total chaos.

19:41.000 --> 19:45.020
But because the box is sealed and there are a finite number of molecules,

19:45.400 --> 19:52.020
the system can only explore a finite, albeit mind-bogglingly large, number of arrangements.

19:52.480 --> 19:52.880
Right.

19:52.880 --> 20:02.540
If you wait long enough, pure statistical probability dictates that all the molecules will eventually bounce their way back into the exact same corner they started in.

20:02.540 --> 20:11.680
It's the idea that given infinite time, every possible combination of a finite system will be played out, which guarantees repetition.

20:12.180 --> 20:12.580
Exactly.

20:12.580 --> 20:15.640
But how does that apply to the entire universe?

20:16.040 --> 20:17.460
The universe is expanding.

20:17.660 --> 20:20.060
It doesn't seem like a sealed glass box.

20:20.820 --> 20:22.920
That is where the cosmological constant comes in.

20:24.100 --> 20:27.420
Our universe is dominated by dark energy, which drives the expansion.

20:27.920 --> 20:30.860
But this expansion actually creates a cosmic horizon.

20:31.360 --> 20:34.100
A boundary beyond which we can never see or interact.

20:34.100 --> 20:39.540
Because of this horizon, the observable universe operates mathematically like a bounded system.

20:40.360 --> 20:44.820
And the relevant entropy of this bounded system is called the de Sitter horizon entropy.

20:45.280 --> 20:46.440
And how big is that entropy?

20:47.240 --> 20:51.260
In Planck units, which are the fundamental quantum pixel sizes of reality,

20:51.940 --> 20:56.920
the de Sitter horizon entropy is estimated to be roughly 10 to the power of 122.

20:57.340 --> 20:59.660
A one followed by 122 zeros.

21:00.180 --> 21:03.920
That number is so large it loses all physical meaning in the human brain.

21:03.920 --> 21:06.020
It is incomprehensibly massive.

21:06.860 --> 21:10.580
And using Kac's lemma, which puts a time frame on Poincaré's theorem,

21:11.100 --> 21:14.160
the formal recurrence time for our universe...

21:14.160 --> 21:18.180
The time it would take for the cosmos to completely cycle through every possible state

21:18.180 --> 21:20.760
and reset to its exact original configuration.

21:21.160 --> 21:21.460
Right.

21:21.660 --> 21:28.820
That time is proportional to the mathematical constant e raised to the power of 10 to the 122.

21:29.040 --> 21:29.660
Okay, wait a minute.

21:29.760 --> 21:30.720
I have to stop you there.

21:31.020 --> 21:31.400
Too big.

21:31.400 --> 21:33.280
The math is bordering on the absurd.

21:33.920 --> 21:37.560
E to the power of 10 to the 122 Planck times.

21:37.780 --> 21:44.680
That time scale is vastly, hilariously longer than the 13.8 billion years our universe has existed.

21:45.000 --> 21:46.120
It's quite a bit longer, yes.

21:46.500 --> 21:51.600
I mean, the paper itself notes that our current universe is only about 10 to the 61 Planck times old.

21:51.860 --> 21:52.220
Right.

21:52.220 --> 22:01.020
If the IT department needs E to the 10 to the 122 years to reboot the system and recover the memory, the theory is dead.

22:01.120 --> 22:04.700
The mechanism relies on a time scale that hasn't even begun to happen yet.

22:04.700 --> 22:11.100
That is an excellent pushback, and it highlights a critical distinction in the paper between theoretical limits and operational mechanics.

22:11.540 --> 22:11.960
How so?

22:12.420 --> 22:20.920
You are looking at the E to the 10 to the 122 number as an operational requirement, but the mathematics treat it strictly as a supremum.

22:21.300 --> 22:22.020
A supremum.

22:22.020 --> 22:24.240
Meaning a strict mathematical upper bound.

22:24.480 --> 22:24.740
Yes.

22:25.560 --> 22:30.420
The recurrence time is simply a formal logical guarantee that the system is fundamentally bounded.

22:30.760 --> 22:31.480
Oh, okay.

22:31.900 --> 22:37.900
It proves mathematically that the information deposited in the CMB record is not bleeding out into absolute infinity.

22:38.360 --> 22:42.340
It is trapped within the system and is therefore theoretically recoverable.

22:42.340 --> 22:47.180
So it provides the legal framework under the laws of physics for memory retention?

22:47.600 --> 22:48.400
Yes, exactly.

22:48.620 --> 22:48.840
Okay.

22:48.940 --> 22:53.220
So it's just the proof of concept that the hard drive will never physically disintegrate.

22:53.440 --> 22:58.800
But how does the universe actually access the memory today in a reasonable time frame?

22:59.180 --> 23:04.820
The operational second-by-second mechanism relies on what the author calls exponentially decaying kernels.

23:05.160 --> 23:05.600
Kernels.

23:05.600 --> 23:12.840
These are the mathematical functions that dictate how strongly the active fields interact with a boundary record over time.

23:13.300 --> 23:24.560
Because these kernels decay exponentially, the effective memory time, the time it takes for the local field to read the memory and adjust, is vastly, vastly shorter than the absolute recurrence time.

23:25.060 --> 23:32.820
So if the recurrence theorem is the guarantee that the hard drive lasts forever, the exponentially decaying kernels act as the RAM.

23:33.060 --> 23:33.400
The RAM.

23:33.400 --> 23:38.240
Yeah, the working memory that the universe accesses locally and continuously in real time.

23:38.680 --> 23:40.520
That is an excellent refinement of the analogy.

23:40.820 --> 23:49.860
The local physical processes are interacting with the deep memory on standard observable cosmological timescales, perfectly avoiding the infinite delays of the supremum.

23:50.260 --> 23:53.300
So the memory is safe, and it can be accessed quickly.

23:53.580 --> 23:53.860
Right.

23:54.160 --> 23:56.600
But how do these fields actually move?

23:57.120 --> 24:02.400
How does a mathematical field physically iron out the wrinkles in the universe without tearing the fabric?

24:02.400 --> 24:06.100
This brings us to the operational mechanics, the math of memory.

24:06.240 --> 24:07.120
Let's get into it.

24:07.320 --> 24:13.320
To understand the motion of the universe in this model, we have to look at gradient flows and free energy.

24:13.600 --> 24:13.880
Okay.

24:14.360 --> 24:18.220
In all physical systems, nature is fundamentally lazy.

24:19.000 --> 24:22.020
It always wants to move toward its lowest possible energy state.

24:22.360 --> 24:23.140
Water flows downhill.

24:23.140 --> 24:25.780
Hot coffee cools down to room temperature.

24:26.060 --> 24:27.260
The path of least resistance.

24:27.620 --> 24:28.020
Exactly.

24:28.980 --> 24:38.540
In the RSVP framework, the entire system of fields is governed by a highly complex prescribed free energy functional, denoted in the paper by a script F.

24:38.780 --> 24:39.520
Script F.

24:39.520 --> 24:46.280
You can imagine this functional as an incredibly complex, multidimensional, topographical landscape of hills and valleys.

24:46.860 --> 24:51.500
The fields are constantly trying to roll down into the deepest valley to reach stability.

24:51.940 --> 24:52.480
Makes sense.

24:52.480 --> 25:02.200
But the scalar structure field phi and the auxiliary density field S roll down this landscape using entirely different rules of physics.

25:02.660 --> 25:03.740
How do their rules differ?

25:04.380 --> 25:08.260
The structure field phi follows what is called an L2 gradient flow.

25:08.760 --> 25:12.580
The density field S follows a Wasserstein gradient flow.

25:12.940 --> 25:14.820
So what does this all mean?

25:15.320 --> 25:16.580
We hit the jargon wall.

25:17.260 --> 25:21.200
What is the physical difference between L2 and Wasserstein gradient flows?

25:21.200 --> 25:24.260
An L2 gradient flow is the simplest way to lose energy.

25:24.860 --> 25:36.360
The field looks purely at its immediate, microscopic, local environment, finds the absolute steepest path downward, and alters its value to drop down that path as fast as mathematically possible.

25:36.580 --> 25:37.540
Just going straight down.

25:37.660 --> 25:38.640
Just going straight down.

25:39.260 --> 25:40.640
It doesn't care about the global picture.

25:40.860 --> 25:42.320
It just wants to go down right now.

25:42.660 --> 25:46.300
So L2 flow is exactly like pouring a bucket of water on a bumpy hill.

25:46.720 --> 25:49.320
The water doesn't plan a route to the bottom of the mountain.

25:49.320 --> 25:55.360
Every single drop just blindly follows the steepest local decline it can find, trickling down the immediate slope.

25:55.600 --> 25:55.960
Exactly.

25:56.360 --> 25:58.340
It alters its state point by point.

25:58.960 --> 26:05.080
Now, the Wasserstein gradient flow is fundamentally more complex because it isn't just altering a static value.

26:05.280 --> 26:08.480
It deals with the physical transport of a conserved density.

26:08.700 --> 26:09.000
Right.

26:09.100 --> 26:10.560
Because S doesn't dilute.

26:10.960 --> 26:13.460
Remember, the S field's mass cannot dilute.

26:13.820 --> 26:17.440
It has to physically move its mass from point A to point B.

26:17.440 --> 26:24.340
A Wasserstein flow calculates the optimal transport path to minimize energy globally, not just locally.

26:24.980 --> 26:25.100
Okay.

26:25.200 --> 26:26.740
The water analogy breaks down here.

26:27.100 --> 26:33.260
If L2 is water trickling down a hill, what is a good way to visualize a Wasserstein flow for a conserved density?

26:33.720 --> 26:35.900
Think about the physics of a river delta forming.

26:36.440 --> 26:40.200
Or, perhaps more accurately, the way a slime mold searches for food.

26:40.960 --> 26:42.260
A slime mold.

26:42.260 --> 26:44.560
Yeah, a slime mold is a conserved mass.

26:45.000 --> 26:47.000
It sends out a million tiny tendrils.

26:47.660 --> 26:53.060
When one tendril finds food, the mold doesn't just pull all its mass in a straight line toward the food,

26:53.480 --> 26:55.960
because it might hit obstacles or tear itself apart.

26:56.380 --> 26:56.820
Oh, wow.

26:57.300 --> 27:07.160
Instead, it dynamically calculates the most efficient, highly routed network of tubes to transport its mass across the environment with the least amount of overall energy expenditure.

27:07.580 --> 27:08.520
Oh, that's fascinating.

27:08.520 --> 27:11.460
I was thinking of it like a city traffic grid.

27:12.120 --> 27:12.440
How so?

27:12.860 --> 27:22.560
Well, if a million cars leave a stadium at once, the traffic manager can't just route every single car down the one steepest, shortest road, because that road will instantly jam.

27:22.800 --> 27:22.940
Right.

27:23.160 --> 27:32.820
The manager has to look at the entire global density of cars and route them through complex, overlapping paths so the entire maps of traffic dissipate smoothly.

27:32.820 --> 27:42.840
If we connect this to the bigger picture, the traffic manager analogy is absolutely perfect for describing optimal transport theory, which is the mathematical foundation of Wasserstein metrics.

27:43.260 --> 27:43.760
Very cool.

27:44.240 --> 27:49.320
The system evaluates the entire density S and moves it globally to lower the free energy.

27:50.100 --> 28:02.080
And because Flyxion chose this specific, rigorous combination of L2 and Wasserstein flows, they were able to prove two monumental properties in the paper, Proposition 5.1 and Proposition 5.2.

28:02.080 --> 28:04.680
Break those propositions down for us.

28:04.800 --> 28:05.460
What did they prove?

28:05.940 --> 28:09.560
Proposition 5.1 addresses the positivity of the auxiliary density.

28:10.220 --> 28:13.280
As we established, S must be greater than or equal to zero.

28:13.740 --> 28:14.020
Right.

28:14.180 --> 28:18.700
The error correction code can't drop into negative numbers or the physics break down into nonsense.

28:19.360 --> 28:23.400
The paper proves that because the equation governing S is quasi-linear and parabolic.

28:23.880 --> 28:24.220
Hold on.

28:24.240 --> 28:29.500
You can't just say quasi-linear and parabolic without explaining what that means for the physical space.

28:29.840 --> 28:30.340
Fair point.

28:30.560 --> 28:31.020
Fair point.

28:31.020 --> 28:35.740
In mathematical terms, a parabolic equation governs things like heat diffusion.

28:36.740 --> 28:41.540
If you heat one end of a metal rod, the heat smoothly and predictably spreads to the colder end.

28:41.780 --> 28:43.340
It doesn't jump around erratically.

28:43.600 --> 28:43.800
Okay.

28:44.360 --> 28:47.840
The equations governing S share this smooth, spreading nature.

28:48.560 --> 28:51.800
Because of this structure, the paper invokes the weak maximum principle.

28:52.000 --> 28:52.540
What does that do?

28:53.160 --> 28:55.980
This principle essentially acts as a mathematical floor.

28:55.980 --> 29:06.880
It guarantees that if you start with a positive amount of density, the smooth spreading nature of the flow will never, under any circumstances, allow the density to cross the zero threshold and become negative.

29:06.880 --> 29:10.780
The traffic manager will never magically create negative cars.

29:11.000 --> 29:11.360
Exactly.

29:11.600 --> 29:13.160
That is a crucial safeguard.

29:13.160 --> 29:18.000
A physics model that accidentally generates negative mass during its calculations is dead on arrival.

29:18.880 --> 29:21.200
And what about Proposition 5.2?

29:21.880 --> 29:25.280
Proposition 5.2 proves dissipation in the reference dynamics.

29:25.280 --> 29:37.200
It proves that if you isolate the system and remove all external chaotic forces, this L2 and Wasserstein combination guarantees that the total free energy strictly decreases monotonically.

29:37.580 --> 29:39.340
Monotonically meaning it never goes up.

29:39.500 --> 29:39.680
Right.

29:39.880 --> 29:41.620
It always predictably settles downward.

29:41.620 --> 29:52.260
So the interaction of these two flows, the water trickling down the hill and the traffic manager routing the cars, ensures that the universe doesn't wildly vibrate itself to pieces trying to fix its errors.

29:52.900 --> 29:55.320
It's a mathematically stable, calming process.

29:56.100 --> 29:58.800
The ironing of the tablecloth is smooth and deliberate.

29:59.220 --> 30:01.040
It is fundamentally stable.

30:01.580 --> 30:08.420
However, the author is incredibly thorough and notes that the actual universe is an open system, not a perfectly isolated one.

30:08.600 --> 30:08.840
Right.

30:08.920 --> 30:10.100
The real universe is messy.

30:10.100 --> 30:23.040
The full equations include what are called non-variational terms, things like a collapse term, a homogeneous source that continuously injects influence, and entropy production driven by the steep gradients of the structure field.

30:23.300 --> 30:27.460
So the universe isn't just settling into a dead flat line of zero energy.

30:27.680 --> 30:34.420
It is dynamically juggling all these messy chaotic inputs while the gradient flows constantly force it back towards stability.

30:34.920 --> 30:35.540
Exactly.

30:35.540 --> 30:41.040
It is a dynamic equilibrium, which leads directly to our next major question.

30:41.740 --> 30:49.320
If the system is constantly being forced towards stability by the memory of the CMB record, what exactly does the final output look like?

30:49.880 --> 30:51.560
What shape does the universe settle into?

30:51.560 --> 30:56.740
To figure this out, Flyxion performs an expansion around a homogeneous background.

30:57.300 --> 31:09.300
Essentially, they imagine a perfectly flat, dead baseline where the structure field, phi, is exactly zero, and the density field, s, is just a constant, unmoving average called s-bar.

31:09.300 --> 31:21.220
And once they establish that dead flat baseline, they introduce tiny microscopic fluctuations, mathematical ripples, to see how the gradient flows and the memory mechanism react.

31:21.700 --> 31:24.480
Like dropping a pebble into a completely still pond.

31:24.840 --> 31:25.340
Precisely.

31:25.340 --> 31:33.260
The first thing they discover is detailed in Proposition 6.1, which establishes the strict rule for spectral stability.

31:33.420 --> 31:34.000
What's the rule?

31:34.200 --> 31:45.920
They find that a specific mode, a specific ripple in the universe, is only stable if the natural damping of the scalar field dominates the non-local feedback of the memory system.

31:46.120 --> 31:48.700
Let me make sure I am visualizing this correctly.

31:48.900 --> 31:54.500
The natural damping is the water in the pond physically resisting the ripple and trying to smooth it out.

31:54.500 --> 31:55.180
Yes.

31:55.740 --> 32:05.080
The non-local feedback is the IT department, the memory system, reading the backup file, realizing a ripple shouldn't be there, and actively intervening to push the water back down.

32:05.440 --> 32:07.540
That is a highly accurate visualization.

32:08.480 --> 32:17.860
So, if the IT department is too aggressive, if the feedback strength is higher than the natural resistance of the water, the system will overcorrect.

32:18.040 --> 32:18.300
Right.

32:18.300 --> 32:29.480
It will push the water down too hard, creating a deeper trough, which requires a bigger correction, and the ripple will rapidly spiral out of control into a massive reality-breaking wave.

32:29.740 --> 32:31.520
That is exactly what the math shows.

32:31.520 --> 32:38.800
The formal condition is gamma rho is greater than or equal to eta eta phi g0 squared.

32:39.480 --> 32:40.480
And in plain English?

32:41.160 --> 32:44.360
Gamma and rho represent the natural damping and energy loss.

32:45.160 --> 32:50.000
The eta terms represent the coupling strengths, how hard the memory system pushes back.

32:50.740 --> 32:55.940
The physics only survive if the natural damping is greater than or equal to the memory's intervention.

32:56.520 --> 33:00.340
Nature has to act as a shock absorber for its own error correction system.

33:00.340 --> 33:00.680
Right.

33:01.700 --> 33:06.200
Okay, assuming the system meets that criteria and remains stable, what happens to the ripple?

33:06.700 --> 33:10.800
The system processes the ripple and produces a stationary response.

33:11.280 --> 33:16.040
In engineering terms, it generates a transfer function denoted as T sub k.

33:16.240 --> 33:17.260
And what does that do?

33:17.500 --> 33:29.040
You can think of this transfer function as a mathematical filter that takes the input, the fossilized CMB record, and maps it directly to the output, which is the physical field of the universe we see today.

33:29.180 --> 33:29.880
It's the echo.

33:29.880 --> 33:30.440
The echo.

33:30.800 --> 33:30.980
Yeah.

33:31.100 --> 33:42.320
The early universe shouts the CMB record into the canyon of time, and the transfer function dictates exactly what that shout sounds like when it bounces back to our telescopes 13.8 billion years later.

33:42.560 --> 33:43.680
I like the echo analogy.

33:43.680 --> 33:56.160
And to measure the fidelity of that echo, how perfectly the output matches the original input, the author introduces Proposition 6.2, which defines a coherence metric labeled C.

33:56.160 --> 34:01.020
Now, I want to jump in here and point out a massive trap that I fell into while reading this section.

34:01.380 --> 34:02.020
Oh, what's that?

34:02.020 --> 34:09.640
Well, you might think a high coherence metric means the universe perfectly recovered all its lost information, like a fully restored hard drive.

34:10.080 --> 34:14.820
You hear coherence metric, and you immediately assume that a high score is the ultimate goal.

34:14.820 --> 34:18.860
You think, ah, a coherence metric of 1 means perfect correlation.

34:19.380 --> 34:24.820
And that assumption is entirely wrong, which is why Flyxion spends significant time dismantling it.

34:24.820 --> 34:31.880
A high coherence metric does not mean the dynamics perform some profound, intelligent recovery of lost physics.

34:32.360 --> 34:33.060
Explain why.

34:33.260 --> 34:34.960
Why isn't a perfect match a victory?

34:35.520 --> 34:40.320
Because a coherence metric of 1 simply means the dynamic filter is completely flat.

34:40.940 --> 34:50.540
A flat filter preserves the basic spatial phases of the input, but it doesn't necessarily recreate the amplitude, the complexity, or the deep physical interactions.

34:50.540 --> 34:55.000
It merely takes the shape of the input and blindly spits it back out.

34:55.320 --> 34:55.440
Yes.

34:55.860 --> 35:01.100
As the paper points out, alignment with the template is trivially built into the basic equations.

35:01.880 --> 35:04.740
It's the difference between an artist and a copy machine.

35:05.120 --> 35:13.880
If I take a transparency of the Mona Lisa and trace the lines with a Sharpie, the spatial alignment of my tracing is mathematically a perfect match to the original.

35:14.260 --> 35:16.200
I would get a coherence metric of 1.

35:16.480 --> 35:18.160
But you didn't actually paint the Mona Lisa.

35:18.480 --> 35:19.760
You just copied the geometry.

35:19.760 --> 35:20.240
Exactly.

35:20.540 --> 35:22.620
That is a superb way to articulate it.

35:23.580 --> 35:31.160
To prove that the model isn't just acting as a dumb copy machine, Flyxion demands three highly rigorous mathematical control tests.

35:31.600 --> 35:32.180
What are they?

35:32.620 --> 35:40.480
First, unforced evolution, where the memory input is turned off entirely to ensure the universe naturally decays into randomness without it.

35:40.880 --> 35:41.380
Makes sense.

35:41.760 --> 35:47.920
Second, ordinary diffusion, where the damping is removed to see if the structure just smears out into a blur.

35:48.260 --> 35:48.600
Okay.

35:48.600 --> 35:58.980
And third, randomized templates, where they feed the system absolute garbage data instead of the CMB to prove that the system won't just hallucinate a structured universe out of nothing.

35:58.980 --> 36:04.840
The intellectual rigor required to build safeguards against your own math is staggering.

36:05.640 --> 36:08.900
But this brings us to a massive structural problem.

36:09.080 --> 36:09.460
What's that?

36:09.460 --> 36:25.420
If the linear transfer function, this single echo, is just acting like a flat filter tracing the input, how in the world do we end up with the highly specific, incredibly nuanced, nearly scale-invariant spectrum we observe in reality?

36:25.420 --> 36:29.120
All right. Tracing a single echo is not enough to build the complex galaxies we see.

36:29.640 --> 36:35.500
This is where the paper shifts gears entirely. A single echo is far too simple.

36:36.240 --> 36:41.620
To build the cosmos, the universe must engage in a massive, continuous feedback loop.

36:41.620 --> 36:45.540
This introduces the concept of cyclic reintegration.

36:45.760 --> 36:47.160
Here's where it gets really interesting.

36:47.800 --> 36:50.280
Because we are no longer just doing abstract math.

36:50.520 --> 36:53.680
We are plugging in the exact, measured numbers of our universe.

36:54.420 --> 36:59.400
Flyxion moves the model from a conditional reading of a single event to a recursive reading.

36:59.960 --> 37:06.220
In this advanced view, the output record of one epoch of the universe becomes the foundational input for the next epoch.

37:06.220 --> 37:07.600
Like a loop.

37:07.720 --> 37:15.640
And during that transition, the universe inevitably injects a small amount of fresh quantum randomness, or noise, into the system.

37:16.000 --> 37:22.580
So the output of cycle one becomes the raw material for cycle two, which processes it and feeds it into cycle three.

37:23.000 --> 37:27.560
Yes. Mathematically, this is modeled as a first-order autoregressive process.

37:28.400 --> 37:32.720
Mode by mode, wavelength by wavelength, the universe processes its memory,

37:32.720 --> 37:36.440
adds a dash of noise, and feeds the result back into itself.

37:36.960 --> 37:40.820
It's like pointing a video camera at a monitor displaying the camera's own live feed.

37:41.120 --> 37:42.880
Or holding a microphone too close to a speaker.

37:43.340 --> 37:47.060
The signal loops, processes, and amplifies its own imperfections.

37:47.240 --> 37:50.680
But in this case, the gradient flows act as the volume knob,

37:50.960 --> 37:54.300
preventing the screeching feedback and forcing the loop to settle down.

37:54.760 --> 37:57.620
That is exactly how autoregressive loops function in physics.

37:57.620 --> 38:03.260
If you run the loop enough times, and the damping is correct, the system eventually reaches a fixed point.

38:03.760 --> 38:09.200
A stable, final, architectural shape that no longer changes, no matter how many more times you run the cycle.

38:09.600 --> 38:09.860
Okay.

38:10.120 --> 38:14.900
So the universe loops its memory millions of times and settles into a final shape.

38:15.060 --> 38:17.240
But we don't have to guess what that shape is.

38:17.240 --> 38:20.480
We look up at the sky, we measure the cosmic microwave background,

38:20.480 --> 38:27.980
and we know that the spectral tilt of our universe, labeled NS, is approximately 0.9649.

38:28.460 --> 38:29.700
Right, from the Planck data.

